(a) whether International trade is the worst victim of the present financial crisis;
(b) if so, what has been the impact of global recession on India`s exports;
(c) the steps taken by Government to revive the export sector;
(d) whether the result so far have not been encouraging; and
(e) if so, the reasons therefor?

THE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY 
(SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA)
(a) & (b): International trade has been impacted adversely due to the global financial crisis and economic slowdown. There has been decline in India’s merchandise exports since October 2008, which can be attributed significantly to shrinkage of demand in developed countries due to global economic slowdown.
(c): To arrest the decline in exports, the Government / RBI announced stimulus packages including the announcements made in the Budget 2009-10 and in the Foreign Trade Policy (FTP) 2009-14 to provide support. Government extended additional support in January 2010. Some of the steps taken by Government and RBI are given in Annexure.
(d) & (e): There has been a progressive reduction in the rate of decline in export growth in dollar terms from May, 2009 to October, 2009 and monthly exports have turned positive since November, 2009; which clearly indicates that all the measures and initiatives taken by the Government and RBI have had a significant impact on effecting a turnaround in our exports.
